WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.283
Your payment service stops answering.

2
00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:06.889
You take a thread dump.

3
00:00:06.889 --> 00:00:10.101
Next to every thread, the JVM prints one word.

4
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:12.473
Which of these threads is doing work?

5
00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:15.808
Each word names a place.

6
00:00:15.808 --> 00:00:16.881
There are six.

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.462
A new thread sits in NEW.

8
00:00:19.462 --> 00:00:20.728
Nothing runs yet.

9
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:23.425
start hands it to the operating system.

10
00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:25.917
Now it is RUNNABLE, and it gets a core.

11
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.310
But cores are few.

12
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.904
When another thread gets the core, ours waits its turn.

13
00:00:30.904 --> 00:00:32.276
It is still RUNNABLE.

14
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.112
So RUNNABLE in a dump does not prove a thread is working.

15
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:40.979
Next, it reaches a synchronized block whose lock another thread holds.

16
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:42.107
Where does it go?

17
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.603
Up to BLOCKED.

18
00:00:44.603 --> 00:00:47.959
When the lock comes free, it takes it and goes back.

19
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:56.263
wait, join or park, with no time limit, puts it in WAITING, until a notify, an unpark, or the joined thread ends.

20
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.427
Now it asks for a ReentrantLock that another thread holds.

21
00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:01.792
Which box this time?

22
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:03.950
WAITING.

23
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:07.958
ReentrantLock is plain Java code that parks the thread.

24
00:01:07.958 --> 00:01:10.363
BLOCKED is only for synchronized.

25
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:16.001
With a time limit, like sleep for two hundred milliseconds, it goes to TIMED_WAITING.

26
00:01:16.001 --> 00:01:17.983
It sleeps here between retries.

27
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.170
Now the service shuts down.

28
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.578
Java cannot kill a thread safely.

29
00:01:22.578 --> 00:01:23.745
It can only ask.

30
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:28.112
interrupt sticks a note on the thread: a flag that says, please stop.

31
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.068
The worker is asleep.

32
00:01:30.068 --> 00:01:31.141
What happens?

33
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:37.893
sleep throws InterruptedException, and the worker runs again, in its catch block.

34
00:01:37.893 --> 00:01:40.101
But sleep took the note off first.

35
00:01:40.101 --> 00:01:41.400
The flag is cleared.

36
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:45.008
If the catch block only logs it, the request is gone.

37
00:01:45.008 --> 00:01:47.492
The loop sleeps again, and never stops.

38
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.190
Rewind.

39
00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.340
This time the catch block puts the note back.

40
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:55.090
The loop checks isInterrupted, sees the note, and returns.

41
00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:00.071
A loop that never blocks must make this check itself, or the note just sits there.

42
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.023
run ends.

43
00:02:01.023 --> 00:02:02.630
TERMINATED is final.

44
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.120
A thread can never start again.

45
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:09.596
In code, it is one line, in Java and in Kotlin.

46
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.757
The rule: interrupt only asks.

47
00:02:11.757 --> 00:02:14.955
Whoever catches it puts the flag back, and stops.

48
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.617
Quick check.

49
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:21.588
A thread waits in tryLock with a one second timeout.

50
00:02:21.588 --> 00:02:22.352
Which box?

51
00:02:24.833 --> 00:02:25.882
TIMED_WAITING.

52
00:02:25.882 --> 00:02:29.028
ReentrantLock parks it, with a time limit.
